 foot morphology\n\nFungal Infection with Systemic Involvement vs Pheochromocytoma:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, blisters, confusion, cough, coughing up blood\n  Favours second: adrenal pheochromocytoma, cerebral hemorrhage, conductive hearing impairment, congestive heart failure, cranial nerve compression\n\nDiabetes Mellitus with Hyperglycaemia vs Cushing's Syndrome:\n  Favours first:  blurred vision, dry mouth, erectile dysfunction, frequent infections, frequent urination\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebellum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal facial shape\n\nDiabetes Mellitus with Hyperglycaemia vs Pheochromocytoma:\n  Favours first:  autosomal dominant inheritance, blurred vision, dry mouth, erectile dysfunction, frequent infections\n  Favours second: adrenal pheochromocytoma, cerebral hemorrhage, chest pain, conductive hearing impairment, congestive heart failure\n\nCushing's Syndrome vs Pheochromocytoma:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebellum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal facial shape\n  Favours second: adrenal pheochromocytoma, cerebral hemorrhage, chest pain, conductive hearing impairment, congestive heart failure\n\nRelated to Diabetes Mellitus with Hyperglycaemia: diabetes mellitus (disease), diabetes mellitus, noninsulin-dependent, diabetic ketoacidosis\n\nRelated to Cushing's Syndrome: acth-dependent cushing syndrome, acth-independent cushing syndrome, adrenal gland disease\n\nRelated to Pheochromocytoma: sporadic pheochromocytoma/secreting paraganglioma\n\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.63):]\n  Source: Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state: a historic review of the clinical presentation, diagnosis, and treatment. > History and Physical\n  If an infectious process precedes HHS, signs and symptoms include:\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.79):]\n  Source: HLAs in Autoimmune Di